        545.8895874023438,
        251.3194580078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ρ =",
      "bbox": [
        306.7201232910156,
        252.0877685546875,
        322.861083984375,
        262.0504150390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "R",
      "bbox": [
        326.1603088378906,
        248.1923828125,
        330.8626708984375,
        280.9993591308594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "xdP(x) also belongs to H for all ρ ∈M. Thus, H",
      "bbox": [
        334.44000244140625,
        249.069091796875,
        545.6485595703125,
        262.3592529296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "contains M. By choosing ν = ν∗, we obtain the tightest",
      "bbox": [
        306.7200927734375,
        259.9893798828125,
        545.889892578125,
        273.279541015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "half-space deﬁned by the normal vector ω that includes M.",
      "bbox": [
        306.72003173828125,
        270.9092712402344,
        545.8507080078125,
        284.1994323730469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "■",
      "bbox": [
        306.72003173828125,
        285.59539794921875,
        314.46099853515625,
        292.4397277832031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It can be observed that (28) reduces to (26) when ω = ei",
      "bbox": [
        306.72003173828125,
        303.0689697265625,
        545.3378295898438,
        316.4225158691406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "for i = 1, . . . , n, where ei is an element of the natural",
      "bbox": [
        306.72039794921875,
        314.10919189453125,
        546.0103759765625,
        328.8392028808594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "basis of Rn. Note that, in Problem (28): (i) the optimization",
      "bbox": [
        306.7205810546875,
        325.0290832519531,
        545.942626953125,
        338.3192443847656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "variables are the amount of non-negative mass assigned to",
      "bbox": [
        306.7205505371094,
        335.948974609375,
        545.7017822265625,
        349.2391357421875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "each point x in X (i.e., the measure Pr(x)); (ii) the objective",
      "bbox": [
        306.7205505371094,
        346.8688659667969,
        545.984130859375,
        360.1590270996094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "function and the constraint are linear in the optimization",
      "bbox": [
        306.7204895019531,
        357.9090576171875,
        545.9420776367188,
        371.19921875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "variables. Therefore, (28) is a semi-inﬁnite linear program",
      "bbox": [
        306.7204895019531,
        368.8289489746094,
        546.0714111328125,
        382.1191101074219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(i.e., inﬁnite number of decision variables but ﬁnite number",
      "bbox": [
        306.7204895019531,
        379.74884033203125,
        545.9580688476562,
        393.03900146484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of constraints). By exploiting duality of semi-inﬁnite linear",
      "bbox": [
        306.72052001953125,
        390.7890319824219,
        545.8380737304688,
        404.0791931152344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "program (see for instance [43]), we can write the dual of",
      "bbox": [
        306.72052001953125,
        401.70892333984375,
        545.8387451171875,
        414.99908447265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(28), which is deﬁned as:",
      "bbox": [
        306.72052001953125,
        412.6288146972656,
        409.0901184082031,
        425.9189758300781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ν∗= inf",
      "bbox": [
        365.16070556640625,
        435.0492248535156,
        398.79888916015625,
        446.850341796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ν ν",
